musb, tusb6010, n8x0: peripheral mode with a built-in USB host

The MUSB model only did host mode, so a guest USB gadget (the N810 as a
USB device, e.g. g_ether) had nobody to talk to. Add peripheral mode
with a "gadget host" playing the PC at the other end of the cable:

- When the gadget connects (POWER.SOFTCONN) it raises VBUS and session
  as a B-device (ID pin floating, mini-B plug), resets the bus after
  100 ms and enumerates at full speed: device descriptor, SET_ADDRESS,
  every configuration, then SET_CONFIGURATION for the first one with a
  CDC ECM function and SET_INTERFACE alt 1 on its data interface.
- EP0 follows the peripheral CSR0 handshake (SVDRXPKTRDY, TXPKTRDY and
  DATAEND, SENDSTALL/SENTSTALL); bulk IN packets are collected into
  Ethernet frames (a short packet ends one), OUT frames are split into
  max-packet chunks with a zero-length packet after a whole number.
- The frames go to a QEMU NIC on the TUSB6010. On n810/n800,
  "-usb -net nic,model=tusb6010 -net user,..." enables it; without it
  nothing changes (ID grounded, host mode as before).

IN packets are as long as the TUSB6010 is told (XFR_SIZE); the FIFO is
written in 32-bit words. Writing EP0's FIFO no longer sets bit 1 of
CSR0 (FIFONOTEMPTY on TX endpoints is TXPKTRDY on EP0).

Also: VBUS_SENSE follows VBUS_VALID; EP0 has its FIFO at offset 0;
FIFO addresses cover the whole 32 KiB FIFO RAM; FIFO accesses to an
endpoint without a FIFO are ignored instead of dereferencing NULL.

QEMU 9.1.3: The gadget-host prototypes go in hw/usb/hcd-musb.h; NET_CLIENT_DRIVER_NIC,
qemu_new_nic() with the reentrancy guard, device_class_set_props(); 9.1
has no nd_table, so n8x0_usb_setup() asks qemu_configure_nic_device()
("-net nic,model=tusb6010" or "-nic user,model=tusb6010").

